 # Tools (`tools/`)
+
+## Early source-execution evidence
+
+For the local source-checkout diagnostic profile, `tools/authority` starts the
+host with isolated, no-site Python and then launches a fresh recorded child. It
+can dispatch the existing targets with `tools/authority tools ...`,
+`tools/authority runtime ...`, `tools/authority foundry ...`,
+`tools/authority server ...`, or `tools/authority test ...`. The test route is
+serial and disables automatic plugin loading; explicitly selected plugins and
+conftest plugins are not qualified by this profile. `tools/authority diagnose` prints the owner’s
+unsealed source-execution diagnostic and local profile measurements.
+
+This entry uses a tracked checkout and its existing `.venv`; it compares selected
+host inputs against the current Git tree and compares each actually loaded
+selected source to its Git bytes in the recorder. The Git tree name is not a
+worktree-wide data-file verification; test files and non-Python runtime data are
+not included in this source-capture profile. The shell passes a minimal environment, so services needing
+other configuration require a separately admitted configuration path. Direct
+console scripts and `python -m` remain candidate routes. Neither this launcher,
+its diagnostic, nor the four measured wheel-member relationships appoints a
+complete runtime profile or enables N9 authority. Builtin/native provenance, the
+full relevant provider graph, material state, protected publication, and
+existing-scope continuity remain unresolved.
 
 ## Purpose
 
